      "id": 1,
      "text": "Brady was named after Brady Creek, which runs through town, after settlers arrived in the 1870s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brady%2C_Texas",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Brady calls itself the \"Heart of Texas\" because it sits just 15 miles from the state’s geographic center.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brady%2C_Texas",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Romanesque Revival McCulloch County Courthouse, a landmark of distinctive stonework, was restored through the Texas Historic Courthouse Preservation Program.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://texastimetravel.com/cities/brady/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Heart of Texas Historical Museum tells stories of cattle drives, a 1942 pilot training school, and a WWII German POW camp.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://texastimetravel.com/cities/brady/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Founded midway between Fort Mason and Fort Griffin, Brady’s location on a frontier military road helped it thrive in early Texas history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://texastimetravel.com/cities/brady/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Country music legend Norma Jean performed on The Porter Wagoner Show and the Grand Ole Opry while living in Brady as a child.",
